#### d.  Which variable(s), if any, reduce the odds of having a 10-year risk of future coronary heart disease? Explain your reasoning.

Since none of the coefficients are negative values in the model summary, there are no variables that reduce the odds of having a 10-year risk of future coronary heart disease. This is because the coefficients are the powers e is raised to in solving for odds. Any positive value raised to e would produce positive/increasing odds.

#### e.  We now move on to answering research question 2 (How accurately can we predict 10-year risk of future coronary heart disease?). One way of doing this is to assess how well the predictions work for new data (i.e. FramVal). Using the model based on the FramTrain dataset (built in Problem 1b), obtain predictions for each patient in FramVal using a threshold of 0.5. Use the model from 1b to obtain predictions. Report the confusion matrix and find the following

#### -   the sensitivity of the test.
#### -   the specificity of the test.
#### -   the accuracy of the test.
#### -   the recall of the test.
#### -   the precision of the test.
#### -   the true negative rate of the test.

```{r}
# Get probabilites
FramVal$pred_prob <- predict(logModel1, newdata = FramVal, type = "response")

#Define threshold
FramVal$pred_threshold <- ifelse(FramVal$pred_prob >= 0.5, 1, 0)

#Get confusion matrix
FramVal$TenYearCHD <- as.factor(FramVal$TenYearCHD)
FramVal$pred_threshold <- as.factor(FramVal$pred_threshold)

table(FramVal$TenYearCHD, FramVal$pred_threshold)
```

Sensitivity of test (TP / (TP+FN)) = 5.6%
Specificity of test (TN / (TN+FP)) = 99.0%
Accuracy of the test ((TP + TN) / Total) = 84.9%
Recall of the test (TP / (TP+FN)) = 5.6%
Precision of the test (TP / (TP + FP)) = 50%
True negative rate of the test (TN / (TN+FP)) = 99%

